- **Format:** structured JSON-per-line. `on_event` formats into a thread-local buffer and makes one `write_all` per line on an append-mode handle, which meets the arch "one `write` per line" rule. The line is codes-only and stays well under 4 KiB. Every role file can have concurrent writers in separate processes, and the same rule covers all of them:
  - `hook-<name>.ndjson`, shared by hook processes;
  - `mcp.ndjson`, shared by one `viola mcp` per Claude session (as landed, chunk 2026-10-17-mcp-server-for-drivers: its self pair, the Tokio client's `channel-*` lines, a refused `send`'s client-side `send-refused`, and from the `list` tool the `agents-probe` pair and the board's `state-recovered` / `parse-rejected` lines);
  - `cli-<name>.ndjson`, shared by concurrent short-lived verbs;
  - `run-<name>.ndjson`, briefly: a second `viola run <name>` or a `viola revive <name>` on a live or `stale` name, opens the same file at init step 4. It appends its `process-start` and its exit-1 `process-exit` (`detail:"already-live"` for a live holder or a fresh replayed beat, Scenario 1; `detail:"stale-heartbeat"` for a `stale` holder) next to the holder's lines. A `gone` name is taken over by `run` instead, with no exit-1 line; a `viola revive` on a `gone` name appends its own lines to the same file and either starts the child or ends with an exit-1 `process-exit` (`no-session`, `session-live`, `cwd-missing`), its `session-live` reading adding one `agents-probe` spawn pair to that file (chunk 2026-10-16-unwrapped-sessions-on-the-board); a revive whose instance check refuses does not open the file at all (chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor). The concurrent-append check covers this collision path too (D-29);
  - the instance detail files `detail-hook.ndjson`, `detail-cli.ndjson` and `detail-mcp.ndjson`, shared by those same processes. Their lines (chains, drift reports, one-line backtraces) are not codes-only and can exceed 4 KiB, so the size argument below does not apply to them. They rely only on the single `write_all` and the torn-line tolerance, and the concurrent-append check must include a detail line larger than 4 KiB (D-28). As landed for the hook files: the check (8 concurrent `viola hook` processes: 16 whole lines in `hook-<name>.ndjson`, 8 in `detail-hook.ndjson`, on CI: then all 3 OS legs, now `ubuntu-latest` alone) carries no line over 4 KiB, and its over-4 KiB half is `hook_panics_append_whole_lines_over_4_kib_side_by_side`: 8 concurrent forced panics through the `fake-agent` seam `FAKE_AGENT_HOOK_PANIC` leave 8 whole role lines and 8 whole `detail-hook.ndjson` lines each over 4 096 B, on CI: then all 3 OS legs, now `ubuntu-latest` alone (chunk 2026-09-28-hook-perf-gate). The panic payload + backtrace is the only hook detail line that large.

  Each line is one `write_all` of one complete line on an append-mode handle. POSIX `O_APPEND` fixes the offset per write. Non-interleaving at this size is an assumption, not a POSIX guarantee, because the 4 KiB `PIPE_BUF` rule applies to pipes. Two things back it:
  - readers tolerate a torn line: the harness emits `{"torn":true}`, and G2 skips it through `fromjson?`;
  - a tests-owned concurrent-append check on `ubuntu-latest` asserts that every line of each shared file parses. This is a multi-platform-exporter-compat item. A torn line fails that check.

